  "query_preview": "period of unsettled weather, but an additional factor\nto consider is a plume of deep tropical moisture, associated\nwith TS Bertha roughly 750 mi to our WSW, forecast to advect \nand pool ahead of the front resulting in PWATs north of 2.25\"\nacross much of the area.\n\nWith the aforementioned synoptic setup, as well as model\nsoundings depicting a deep warm cloud layer, ample instability\nbut skinny CAPE profiles, and likely training storm motions, the\nlocal environment is primed for a flash flooding threat despite\nlong-term antecedent drought conditions. Timing of rainfall\nonset remains uncertain with a decent amount of spread still\npresent in CAMS, although general consensus is the threat\nbeginning overnight Wednesday along and north of Highway 64,\ngradually filling in to the south into the day Thursday.\nGuidance points to a drier airmass intruding into northern\nzones late Thursday night, but deeper moisture remains pooled\nsouth of this line and especially over the Crystal Coast through\nFriday-Saturday and potentially longer.\n\nForecast storm total amounts for the area currently range from\n4-6\" through Saturday night. The most likely period for the\nmost intense rainfall is Thursday into Thursday night, and this\nis supported by the ECMWF EFI which continued its trend from \nyesterday of exceeding the 80-90% percentile of forecast \nprecipitation. SOT values of 1-2 point to some risk for a\nhigher-end event beyond what is forecast. It should be noted\nthat since this will be a convective episode, showers and\nthunderstorms will be hit-and-miss through the period in\ncontrast to a total washout. That being said, the environment\nwill be highly favorable for rainfall rates of 2-3\" an hour,\npotentially higher in more robust thunderstorms. In urban areas\nor highly vulnerable locations, this could quickly bring\nflooding concerns.\n\nDespite uncertainty in frontal progression timing, the highly \nfavorable environment for flash flooding necessitated a Flood\nWatch for the entire FA. Areas along and north of Highway 64 are\nvalid until 12Z Friday, while areas south are valid until 00z\nSaturday. Extensions are possible into the weekend, especially\nalong the southern coast. WPC maintains our area in a Slight\n(Level 2/4) risk of excessive rainfall for tonight, and a\nModerate (Level 3/4) risk on Thursday. Slight risk remains for\nthe area into Friday and Saturday as well.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The front tonight will be accompanied by an \nuptick in shear, although there will be poor forcing across our \narea as well as potentially limited instability with lingering \ncloud cover. Still, CAMs and AI NWP point to a marginal risk of \na stronger storm or two, most likely across southeastern VA but \npotentially extending into the Albemarle Peninsula and areas \nnorth of Highway 264. Most likely timing is after 20z with \ndamaging wind gusts being the primary threat, but an isolated \ntornado is possible as surface winds back ahead of the \napproaching front.",
